post("soc-2-type-ii-security-platform", "Software and cloud",
     "What does SOC 2 Type II mean for a security platform?",
     "What SOC 2 Type II means for a security platform",
     "What our annual SOC 2 Type II examination covers: Dealer Admin and Virtual Keypad, who examines them, and which reports a customer can see.",
     "We share what our own examination covers: Dealer Admin and Virtual Keypad, examined every year.",
     img("vault-1200.jpg", "A glowing shield with a padlock", 800, 600),
     "A SOC 2 Type II examination is an independent look at a company's controls. We complete one every year, conducted by a licensed CPA firm to AICPA standards, for Dealer Admin and Virtual Keypad. The SOC 3 report is intended for public distribution, and SOC 2 reports go to customers and partners under confidentiality.",
     [sec("What does our SOC 2 Type II examination cover?",
          "It covers Dealer Admin and Virtual Keypad, our SaaS applications, which are hosted within a Tier 4 data center. Tier 4 is designed to host mission-critical servers with fully redundant subsystems and compartmentalized security zones. Dealer Admin is a cloud-based portal for managing a dealer's business and customers' DMP panels. Virtual Keypad lets customers manage their systems from a computer, tablet or smartphone. For the formal definition of the criteria, the AICPA is the authority, so this article speaks only to what we have stated about our own examinations.",
          ["Tier 4 means fully redundant cooling, power, network links and storage",
           "Both are examined independently each year",
           "Compartmentalized security zones are part of the Tier 4 design",
           "Dealer Admin has a Single Sign-On option and Custom Roles for staff",
           "This article makes no claim about other products"]),
      sec("Who examines it, and how often?",
          "A licensed CPA firm conducts the examination, in accordance with standards established by the American Institute of Certified Public Accountants (AICPA). We complete it annually, so a dealer or building owner is not relying on a single result from the past.",
          ["Independent: a licensed CPA firm conducts it",
           "Annual: we complete an examination every year",
           "Reports: a public SOC 3 report and confidential SOC 2 reports",
           "Standards: those established by the AICPA"], flip=True, alt=True),
      {"type": "comparison", "heading": "What is covered, at a glance?", "intro": "These are the facts we state about our examination.",
       "columns": ["Question", "Answer"],
       "rows": [["Which applications?", "Dealer Admin and Virtual Keypad"],
                ["Who examines?", "A licensed CPA firm"],
                ["Under which standards?", "Those established by the AICPA"],
                ["How often?", "Every year"],
                ["Where are they hosted?", "Within a Tier 4 data center"],
                ["Public report?", "The SOC 3 report is intended for public distribution"],
                ["Detailed report?", "SOC 2 reports go to customers and partners under confidentiality"],
                ["What do they do?", "Dealer Admin manages a dealer's business and customers' DMP panels. Virtual Keypad lets customers manage their systems from a computer, tablet or smartphone."]]},
      sec("Who can see the reports?",
          "There are two. The SOC 3 report is intended for public distribution. SOC 2 reports are available to customers and partners under appropriate confidentiality arrangements. That gives a specifier a public document to start with and a confidential one to request. Ask early if a project needs the SOC 2 report, because it is shared under confidentiality arrangements rather than published.",
          ["SOC 3: intended for public distribution",
           "SOC 2: customers and partners, under confidentiality",
           "Inside Sales, 877-757-4367, can route the question"], flip=True, alt=True),
      {"type": "cards", "eyebrow": "The examined software", "heading": "What do Dealer Admin and Virtual Keypad actually do?",
       "items": [["Bulk programming", "Dealer Admin performs bulk remote updates and mass programming changes, with system health status on a single dashboard."],
                 ["Staff authority", "Custom Roles grant staff only the authority they need to serve customers, but nothing more."],
                 ["Single Sign-On", "It enhances security and reduces time spent managing passwords and usernames."],
                 ["Alarm verification", "When a system is in alarm, Virtual Keypad users tap the notification, view live camera footage and confirm Real Alarm or False Alarm."],
                 ["Users and profiles", "On XR Series, the system manager can create up to 99 profiles and assign each user to up to four of them."],
                 ["Temporary users", "Temporary XT and XTL users are deleted automatically after seven days."]]},
      sec("What should a specifier ask for?",
          "A short list turns the examination into something a project can use. Ask which applications the project depends on, then ask for the reports that fit.",
          ["Ask which of our applications the project will use: Dealer Admin, Virtual Keypad or both",
           "Ask for the SOC 3 report, which is intended for public distribution",
           "Ask for the SOC 2 report under confidentiality when the project needs more detail",
           "Ask where the software is hosted: within a Tier 4 data center",
           "Ask about the equipment separately, because product pages carry their own listings and certifications",
           "Ask whether the project will carry phone credentials, which are managed in Dealer Admin and Virtual Keypad"], alt=True),
      sec("What else does a security platform need around it?",
          "A SOC 2 examination speaks to hosted software. Building security also depends on the equipment on the wall. Where our product pages state them, these are the protections around the platform. Each is stated on its own product page.",
          ["263LTE communicators carry NIST AES certification, #2350 128-bit and #2595 256-bit",
           "The XR550 offers encrypted 10/100 network or Wi-Fi communication options",
           "The SCS-1R and SCS-VR receivers carry NIST AES certification",
           "JamAlert is UL 2610 certified",
           "The XR650, coming soon and not yet orderable, lists TLS 1.3, AES encryption and PKI with dual certificate trust domains, with FIPS 140-3, 802.1x network authentication and Active Directory client integration available with licenses"], flip=True, alt=True)],
     [["Dealer Admin", "The cloud portal for managing a dealer's business and customers' panels, with Single Sign-On and Custom Roles.", "products/dealer-admin.html"],
      ["Virtual Keypad", "Remote management for customers on Android, iPhone and the web, with Real Alarm or False Alarm verification.", "products/virtual-keypad.html"],
      ["263LTE Cellular Communicator", "NIST AES certified LTE communication for XT and XR panels, sent directly to the receiver.", "products/263lte.html"],
      ["JamAlert", "UL 2610 certified cellular jamming detection.", "products/jamalert.html"],
      ["Certifications", "Our examinations, listings and approvals in one place.", "about/certifications.html"]],
     [COMMON_FAQ["soc"],
      ["Which applications are covered?", "The Dealer Admin and Virtual Keypad SaaS applications, which are hosted within a Tier 4 data center."],
      ["How often is the examination done?", "Annually, by a licensed CPA firm, in accordance with AICPA standards."],
      ["Who can see the SOC 2 report?", "Customers and partners, under appropriate confidentiality arrangements. The SOC 3 report is intended for public distribution."],
      ["What is Tier 4?", "A data center tier designed to host mission-critical servers with fully redundant subsystems and compartmentalized security zones."]])

# =========================================================================== (f) wireless retrofit
post("wireless-security-devices-retrofit", "Communicators and wireless",
     "What should you know before a wireless security retrofit?",
     "What to know before a wireless security retrofit",
     "Which 1100 Series receiver fits which panel, how the 1100T translator keeps other makers' devices, and how to test wireless signals before you drill.",
     "Two-way, supervised 900 MHz devices add zones without new wire. Test the signal before the first screw.",
     img("p-1101-1200.jpg", "The DMP 1101 universal wireless transmitter", 1200, 933),
     "Pair the right receiver with the panel, keep working devices where you can, and test every location. Our 1100 Series is two-way and supervised on 900 MHz. The 1100T translator learns up to 128 zones from other makers' transmitters, and the panel's wireless test checks signals before you commit.",
     [sec("Which wireless receiver goes with which panel?",
          "The 1100 Series uses two-way, supervised 900 MHz frequency-hopping spread-spectrum technology. The receiver follows the panel: XT30 and XT50 panels use the 1100DH, and XT75, XR150 and XR550 panels use the 1100XH. The XT50 also includes an onboard 1100D Series Wireless Receiver.",
          ["The 1100XH supports up to 500 wireless transmitters",
           "It adds transmit and receive amplification for greater distance or harsh building environments, and mounts near the panel enclosure or in a remote location",
           "With version 210/310 firmware, up to 16 repeaters connect to an XR Series panel, up from eight, and transmitters connect to a nearby receiver or repeater more quickly",
           "Watch for hardwired zones or outputs already programmed on the LX expansion bus"]),
      sec("Can you keep the wireless devices already on the wall?",
          "Yes, for the makers it supports. The 1100T Wireless Translator upgrades non-DMP systems with one-way, low-frequency wireless transmitters, including Interlogix, Honeywell 5800, 2GIG and DSC. It talks to a DMP panel through an 1100 Series receiver's wireless connection, so there is no new wiring.",
          ["One 1100T can learn in up to 128 zones",
           "Existing transmitters keep sending supervision and low battery messages to the panel",
           "The 1100TF adds 24-hour standby battery backup",
           "Certified for commercial and residential smoke detection"], flip=True, alt=True),
      {"type": "comparison", "alt": True, "heading": "How do receivers, key fobs and zone numbers line up by panel?", "intro": "Wireless zone numbers and key fob zones differ by panel, so check them before you program.",
       "columns": ["Panel", "Receiver", "Key fob zones", "Wireless zone numbers"],
       "rows": [["XT30 and XT50", "1100DH", "31 to 34 and 41 to 44", "Not covered here"],
                ["XTLplus and XTLtouch", "Built in, 99 two-way wireless zones", "51 to 54 and 61 to 64", "Not covered here"],
                ["XR150", "1100XH", "400 to 449", "500 to 599"],
                ["XR550", "1100XH", "400 to 449", "500 to 999"]]},
      {"type": "cards", "eyebrow": "Four jobs, four devices", "heading": "Which devices cover the usual retrofit jobs?",
       "items": [["1101 Universal Transmitter", "Two internal magnetic reed switches and an onboard terminal block give two independent zones from one transmitter. It includes a 3V lithium battery (CR123)."],
                 ["1128 Glassbreak Detector", "Fully supervised, low-current shock and glassbreak sensing, up to 20 feet from framed glass mounted in an outside wall."],
                 ["1142 Two-Button Wireless Panic", "Made for installation under the counter, with programmable LED feedback when a panic alarm is sent. The 1142BC version has a belt clip."],
                 ["1164 Smoke Detector", "A battery-powered photoelectric detector with a synchronized sounder. When one sounds, the panel commands all 1164s to sound. Up to 572 units on one system."]]},
      sec("How does a retrofit handle wireless smoke detection?",
          "The 1164 is a battery-powered wireless photoelectric smoke detector with an integrated synchronized sounder. It uses our 900 MHz two-way wireless technology, which has been approved for commercial fire applications since 2009. Any fire zone tripped on the panel makes the sounders sound, and the panel can trigger wired strobes and strobe horns.",
          ["Up to 572 units on one system",
           "Listed to ANSI/UL 268, 7th Edition, with CSFM and FDNY listings",
           "The 1164NS has the same functionality without the built-in sounder, for sites with existing sounders or notification devices",
           "Models are the 1164-W wireless smoke detector and the 1164NS-W with no sounder"], flip=True),
      sec("How do you test the signal before you drill?",
          "Run the panel's wireless test, from a keypad or in Dealer Admin under System Tests, Wireless Test. It starts a five-minute check-in test that checks the consistency of your signals, and at the end it shows which transmitters failed.",
          ["Zones that fail need to be relocated, or a repeater should be added",
           "The other way is the LED built into every 1100 Series transmitter: with the cover removed, press the tamper switch and look for five consecutive blinks",
           "If the LED stays on for about eight seconds or flashes several times in quick succession, relocate the transmitter or the receiver"], alt=True),
      sec("What causes the usual surprises after install?",
          "Four things are worth planning for.",
          ["Wireless Trouble means the panel and receiver are not transmitting data back and forth; check that the case tamper and wall tamper on the receiver are depressed",
           "A keypad that stops working after programming may share an address with wireless zones added at the same address",
           "Repeaters are programmed as zones, with sequential zone numbers when there are several",
           "A low battery message clears after you replace the battery and perform a sensor reset",
           "On the XT50, the wireless antenna terminal block is in the top right corner of the circuit board, and the left terminal is not used"], flip=True)],
     [["1100XH Wireless Receiver", "Up to 500 transmitters on XR Series and XT75 panels, with extra amplification for distance.", "products/1100xh.html"],
      ["1100T Wireless Translator", "Take over Interlogix, Honeywell 5800, 2GIG and DSC transmitters, with up to 128 zones and no new wiring.", "products/1100t.html"],
      ["1101 Universal Transmitter", "Two independent zones from one transmitter.", "products/1101.html"],
      ["1128 Glassbreak Detector", "A supervised wireless shock and glassbreak sensor.", "products/1128.html"],
      ["1142 Two-Button Wireless Panic", "Under-the-counter hold up with LED feedback.", "products/1142.html"],
      ["1164 Smoke Detector", "Synchronized sounders, up to 572 units on one system, listed to ANSI/UL 268.", "products/1164.html"]],
     [["How many wireless transmitters can the 1100XH support?", "Up to 500 wireless transmitters."],
      ["Which other makers' transmitters can the 1100T learn?", "Interlogix, Honeywell 5800, 2GIG and DSC one-way, low-frequency wireless transmitters, up to 128 zones per translator."],
      ["How far does the 1128 cover?", "Up to 20 feet from framed glass mounted in an outside wall."],
      ["What battery does the 1142 use?", "One 3V lithium battery (CR123A), included."],
      ["Where do key fob zones start on an XR550?", "Key fobs use zone numbers 400 to 449 on the XR150 and XR550."]])

